@@ -0,0 +1,83 @@
+##############################################################################
+# (c) Crown copyright 2024 Met Office. All rights reserved.
+# The file LICENCE, distributed with this code, contains details of the terms
+# under which the code may be used.
+##############################################################################
+
+# This file contains a list of all linked external repositories containing code
+# and data required to build lfric applications and run the entire test suite
+#
+# Each section is the name of the repository
+# source: Is either the url to the repository (the upstream or
+#         a fork) or a path to a local clone with format `host:/path/to/clone`
+# ref: Is a valid git reference. This can either be a commit hash, a tag or a
+#      branch name
+#
+# Multiple branches can also be set, fork example:
+#
+# lfric_apps:
+#     - source: git@github.com:MetOffice/lfric_apps.git
+#       ref: 2025.12.1
+#     - source: git@github.com:my_fork/lfric_apps.git
+#       ref: my_branch
+#
+# It is up to the user to ensure these merge cleanly
+
+# [isambard3] ADDED. Upstream u-dt000 has no dependencies.yaml: the UniExeter port
+# replaced the Met Office extract with a bespoke `git_extract_lfric` shell app that
+# cloned lfric_apps@vn2.2 / lfric_core@core2.2 and then read the physics revisions
+# out of lfric_apps' own dependencies.sh. This restores the mechanism a Met Office
+# user expects — declare repo@ref here, `merge_sources.py` clones (and merges) it.
+#
+# THE ICE-GIANT FORCING IS NOT DECLARED HERE, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.
+#
+# This suite's science is `theta_forcing='ice_giants_obs_like'`, which exists only
+# on Denis Sergeev's branch
+#
+#     https://github.com/dennissergeev/lfric_apps/tree/ice_giants_tf   (b57ddcc9)
+#
+# and in no MetOffice tag. The obvious thing to write here is the two-source form
+# above — mainline plus his branch, and let merge_sources.py merge them. It does
+# not work: his branch is still based on vn3.0 (he has not had time to upgrade it),
+# and merging it onto 2026.07.1 conflicts in
+# `science/gungho/rose-meta/lfric-gungho/HEAD/rose-meta.conf`, where mainline has
+# since added a 'nudging' option to the same two `values=` lines he added
+# 'ice_giants_obs_like' to. merge_sources.py auto-resolves conflicts only under
+# `rose-stem/` and in `dependencies.yaml`; anything else is a hard error.
+#
+# So the forward-port is carried as a patch instead —
+# `patches/optional/32-lfric_apps-ice-giants-forcing-patch.sh`, applied to the
+# extracted tree by the `git_extract_lfric` task — where the resolution is visible
+# and reviewable, and where the .patch file is directly what to hand back to Denis.
+# When his branch is rebased onto a current LFRic (or the forcing reaches mainline)
+# this file goes back to the two-source form and that patch is deleted.
+#
+# 2026.07.1 is what this repo's environment was built and validated against, and
+# what its vendored submodules are pinned at — so the `mirrors` extract path can
+# serve these refs offline. Building a different ref is the normal upstream edit:
+# change it here (and, for the mirrors path, fetch it into the submodule first —
+# see vendor/mirrors/README.md).
+
+casim:
+    source: git@github.com:MetOffice/casim.git
+    ref: 2026.07.1
+
+jules:
+    source: git@github.com:MetOffice/jules.git
+    ref: 2026.07.1
+
+lfric_apps:
+    source: git@github.com:MetOffice/lfric_apps.git
+    ref: 2026.07.1
+
+lfric_core:
+    source: git@github.com:MetOffice/lfric_core.git
+    ref: 2026.07.1
+
+socrates:
+    source: git@github.com:MetOffice/socrates.git
+    ref: 2026.07.1
+
+ukca:
+    source: git@github.com:MetOffice/ukca.git
+    ref: 2026.07.1
